SkidSheet reads a skid's design parameters off the skid itself: one RO and EDI skid in IsoKit line art on a framed general-arrangement sheet, a numbered tag on a leader from each part to the parameter it governs, and the parameter rows beside the sheet under the same numbers.

- On the site: a `spec` block with `"look": "skid-sheet"` (renderer `assets/src/blocks/look-skid-sheet.js`); live on sys-pw "Skid design parameters" (#design). Pass 12 build round B from concept 224 (`design-system/inspiration/gemini/224-p12-sys-pw-design.png`, audit row 6). Taken from the concept: the GA sheet with its title block, tags on leaders keyed to ruled rows, the phone as drawing then rows. Not taken: its invented tag numbers (they repeat and land on arbitrary parts), the wordmark, the subtitle, metal parts. Each tag is pinned on its governing part from the brief; the copy is the page's own.
- Fields: `h`, `caption` (eyebrow and a title block cell), `intro`, `rows [{p, v, m, link, link_label, part}]`, `note`. `part` (look-only, DR-018) names the drawn part a row governs: `membranes` (capacity), `utilities` (the feed stub: battery limits), `exchanger` (feed heat exchanger: membrane design temperature), `edi` (sanitization), `piping` (product line and its sample valve tee: materials and dead legs), `joints` (the permeate run with a neutral seam ring at each joint; no flanges, so the drawing does not answer `[PP joining method]`), `frame` (the base frame: budget), `pump` (the RO high-pressure pump), `drain` (the reject line: recovery), `panel` (instruments and controls). A row without a part lists with no tag. `fold` is not read: every row shows on every width (pass 12 S7, a tag always has its row).
- Drawing: base frame, RO rack (housings, frame, its vertical multistage pump), plate heat exchanger turned to the feed, EDI stack, control panel; feed stub in at the left, permeate to EDI on a plain run with seam rings at its joints, product out to a stub at the right with a sample valve on a short tee, reject over the front edge to drain. Default light hand (`line-strong` on `surface`), flow on the water lines.
- Markup: `div.aq-skid-sheet[data-pk-sticky]` → `div.__fig[data-pk-fig]` → `div.__sheet` (double rule: 1 px `line-strong` border, `line` outline) → `div.aq-iso-figure.__art` (svg `.__svg`, leaders `g.__ld[data-r]`, `span.__ring`, tags `span.__tag[data-r]` at the leader ends, dots `span.__dot[data-r][data-pk-key]` on the parts) and `div.__block` (title block: page name, caption, count; `aria-hidden`); then `ol.__rows` of `li.__row[data-r][data-pk-row][data-part][tabindex=0]` (`span.__n`, `h3.__p` with a phone-only `span.__pn`, `p.__v`, `button.__wb` (phones), `div.__m` basis and link).
- Desktop: the sheet (7 of 12 columns) holds sticky at `space-24` while the rows (5 of 12) run on hairlines: mono number 13 px, parameter Geist 500 17/24, value 15/22, basis 14/20 `ink-muted`, link in `flow`. Pointing at or focusing a row (or its tag) lights the row (`flow-tint`), its tag (`navy`), leader and part (`flow`), and a ring glides to the tag.
- **Tablets** (601–860 px): the full sheet, tags, leaders and title block, stacked above the rows (no sticky). The motion strikes the marks actually shown (tags here, dots on phones).
- **Phones** (≤ 600 px, `data-pk-bp="600"`): PhoneKit sticky figure (`data-pk-h` 220, `data-pk-zoom` 1.15): no frame or title block, no leaders; the drawing runs full width with a 24 px numbered dot on each part, and the strip pans to the dot of the row on the reading line (that dot turns navy). The rows are a two-column key and value list: number and parameter (15/21) with a 44 px "Basis" button under them, the value (14/21) at the right; the basis and link open under the row. About 1,560 px for ten rows (from 1,966).
- Use it for 6–10 design parameters of one skid where each value belongs to a part. For a tank and its pipework see TankGa; for running costs on a skid, CostSkid; for limits, LimitStack.

## Motion

The sheet draws itself through `AQiso.timeline` (parts back to front, pipes and leaders, the water runs the skid once), then the tags strike in number order at their leader ends (phones: the dots on their parts); rows rise as they scroll in. Once, under 5 s. The hover ring is ported from Subframe Motion "Inside the image" (atlas-hotspots), MIT, (c) 2026 Subframe: it travels 250 ms `cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1)` and the chosen tag darkens in 180 ms. Reduced motion: the finished sheet; the ring jumps without travel.
